  "query_preview": "..(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 404 AM EST SAT NOV 21 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period, a closed upper level low \nwill be located over the Upper Midwest, with an associated surface\nlow in the vicinity of Ontario. A cold front extending from this \nlow will be located in western Kentucky early Monday but is poised\nto quickly push east through the day, even as the system loses \nsome steam as upper level support wanes with time. The environment\nahead of the front will be anomalously moist, with LREF mean PW \nvalues generally in the 0.8 to 1 inch range, representing the 80th\npercentile or higher when compared to climatology; however, this \nmoisture plume will be relatively narrow. Additionally, frontal \nforcing will be relatively weak, and the front will be fast \nmoving, limiting QPF. Even the LREF 90th percentile (a reasonable \nestimate of a \"high end\" solution) keeps QPF with the frontal \npassage below 0.25 inches across the forecast area, with the more \nlikely outcome being less than a tenth of an inch in most \nlocations. \n\nBehind the front, a quieter pattern will briefly set in. \nPostfrontal northwesterly winds will usher in a cooler air mass, \nkeeping temperatures below average, with highs remaining only in \nthe upper 40s to low 50s across the forecast area. Aloft, \nshortwave ridging will translate east into the Plains. Associated \nsurface high pressure will build in the Midwest, with winds \nbecoming more northeasterly throughout the day. This pattern, in \nconjunction with dewpoints in the 20s and low total column \nmoisture (PW values in the 0.2 to 0.3 inch range), will keep skies\nmostly clear and PoPs near zero. However, a more active period of\nweather looks to arrive by midweek.\n\nBy Wednesday, the midlevel pattern looks to become more amplified\nas an expansive upper level low and associated trough push into \nthe West Coast, with midlevel ridging building over the \nIntermountain West. Just east of the ridge, multiple embedded \nwaves will approach the forecast area: one well defined shortwave \nin the southern Plains and another well defined shortwave over the\nUpper Midwest. Deterministic guidance is coming into better \nagreement with the evolution of these features, depicting the \nMidwest wave becoming a closed low before diving southeast along \nthe eastern periphery of the aforementioned Mountain West ridge. \nMeanwhile, guidance depicts the Plains wave lifting northeast \nalong the western periphery of weak midlevel ridging over the \nSoutheast. There is still high uncertainty regarding the strength \nand placement of these features, and therefore it remains unclear \nhow these features will interact; however, there is good agreement\non a general troughing pattern, which would suggest cooler and \nmore active weather locally. NBM PoPs currently peak in the 40-50%\nrange across the forecast area on Wednesday and Thursday, \nalthough additional details are sparse this far out.",
